# My 2025
2025 has been a bit of an oddball year for me. As of Christmas, I've now left two
different jobs in the span of a year, the first time from the first company that ever paid
me a salary and that taught me a lot about the basics of being a SWE, and the second from a
company that taught me that I'm a pretty good engineer, actually, and also that I need to
care about the code I'm writing. I'm now entering a period of a couple of months off to
let myself recover from self-imposed burnout, and to take some time to really figure out
what I actually want from myself. Basically I'm having a miniature quarter-life crisis (at
least I *hope* it's quarter-life, I would really love to live across 3 different
centuries).

> []($block.attrs("author-note"))
> I feel that it is only fair to preface this next bit with an acknowledgement that I'm a
> relatively wealthy straight white dude living as an expat in objectively one of the most
> stable & safe countries on the planet, so I understand that I'm coming from a place of
> privelege. I earned my skill, but the metaphor I use is that I started the 100m sprint
> at the 10m line. Anyway, that doesn't mean I'm not allowed to be human and have emotions
> and write about them, but it would feel disingenuous not to at least call it out.

A little over a month ago, in my [inevitable rant about LLMs](llms-loneliness-and-learning),
I wrote that one of the predominant things I felt this year was loneliness. To be honest,
I'm not really 100% sure why this was (other than the fact that my romantic life this year
was a pretty heavy tumble from 2024). I spent a considerable amount of time with my
friends, both in the Netherlands and back in the States, both virtually and in-person. I
talked to my family often, I took the opportunity to do new things more than in previous
years, I can speak Dutch to a level that is no longer embarrassing (most of the time,
still called a baby "spicy" instead of "cute" by mistake), and I finally got to acutally
travel around Europe this summer.

But still, something this year, especially towards the end of it, just hasn't felt right.
Having stewed on it for the last few weeks, I think it's a combination of a lot of things:
* Being burned out at both jobs, and especially the second one not at all turning out to
be what I thought it would from the interviews.
* I ran less this year than I did in both previous years (the only 2 for which I have
stats, since 2023 is when I really started to get into distance running). Even though I
(just barely) crossed 1000km, my total distance is noticably less than 2024 or 2023 (by 80
and 230km, respectively).
* I feel like I've just been inconsistent at most things through this year. I'll pick
something up for a few weeks or months only to let it slowly be abandoned, then repeat
with something else. This is a big thing I want to work on next year: consistency. I'm
going to talk about that more later on in this essay, but I want to stick to things more.

Anyway, the good news is that the Earth has completed one more loop around the Sun, and
even though the choice of when a "New Year" is may be completely arbitrary, it serves as a
great motivator to think about who I was and who I want to be. And it means everyone and
their mother has a "Year in Review" stats page for you. And I love stats. So let's do some
stats.

[]($section)
# Stats!
I like them. Here are a selection of ones that I care about.

## Personal/Miscellaneous
* As mentioned above, I ran 1001.3km in 2025. Lower than previous years, but at least I still
got the Megameter!
* Of those runs, 2 were marathons: one in Spain with a time of 4:31:41 and one in Scotland
with a time of 4:34:45. Not the time I was hoping for on either, but at least I'm
consistent! And they gave me canned soup at the end of the one in Scotland. I don't know
why.
* My local [wakatime server](https://github.com/muety/wakapi) says I spent 75.2 hours in neovim this
year since August (when I started running it), mostly in markdown (because I keep my notes
in a git repo). Beyond that though, my top 3 languages are, in surprising order, [`gleam`](https://gleam.run/),
[`rust`](https://rust-lang.org/), and [`zig`](https://ziglang.org). To be fair, this is skewed
because I did Advent of Code (or the few days I could find the time) in gleam, and I spent
a good amount of time doing [ziglings](https://codeberg.org/ziglings/exercises) to play
with the language. Combine that with the fact that I tend to just leave multiple projects
open in different tmux windows at once, and I think it's fair to say these stats are
definitely not that accurate. And they don't include code from `$dayjob`, which would
overwhelmingly dominate. Still interesting!
* My personal music indexer-but-also-stats-tracker thing, [eurydice](https://codeberg.org/garrettm/eurydice),
shows my stats since mid-July (when I finally suceeded in getting rid of spotify) with
KGLW (unsurprisingly) dominating:

[Note that the "Total X in Eurydice Collection" rows are a bit misleadingly named. Those
are the total counts that I've played, not the overall in my entire collection (which is
shown in the second table). I need to update the code.]($image.asset('eurydice-stats.png').size(800,0))

## Games
I played a bunch of games this year, though not as many as I'd like! In generally
chronological order:
* **Tactical Breach Wizards** was a fantastically funny and excellently paced
puzzle/strategy game that I played on my Steam Deck in January. Recommend!
* **Sekiro: Shadows Die Twice** is a sad one, in that I DNFed on two separate attempts to
get through it. I *love* the parry mechanic, but the game so often throws something that
breaks that core loop at you that I just got frustrated and gave it up. Perhaps next year
it will stick.
* I've had a **Baldur's Gate 3 (Dark Urge Playthrough)** going with one of my best friends
since the very start of the year. We're very close to done, but coordinating time to play
across two continents is a little bit of a trick. Durge is an absolute blast though, and
shows off so many things I didn't even know existed in the game, even after over 200 hours
of playing it.
* **Haste** was my game of choice while travelling to Spain. It was... fine. The core
gameplay is very fun, but I don't get why it had to be a roguelite, and it gets a bit
stale quickly. Worth what I paid, and a good pickup especially on Deck for a solid few
hours.
* **A Highland Song** was dissapointing. I *love* the art style, and the music is another
highlight (enough so that I tracked down the main band and bought their entire
discography), but the game is just kinda frustrating. Short, thankfully, but a little sad
about this one.
* **A Short Hike** is recommended everywhere as a perfect "cozy" game, and it did not
dissapoint even in the middle of summer. Played it in one shot and had a great little time
with a great little game.
* With Silksong coming out this year, I replayed **Hollow Knight**, this time also doing a
lot of the optional content I skipped on my first go through. Still didn't come anywhere
close to 100%, and I haven't even started Silksong, but it was good to see that I am less
sucky at hard games now.
* The big boy. The one I knew was going to be my main time sink this year. I put 91 hours
into **Hades II** this year, spread among the various early access patches and then a huge
amount on full release. I also 100%ed the achievements on Steam. It lived up to everything
I hoped it would be, and I will die on the hill that it got robbed for Game of the Year at
the Game Awards.
* **Dispatch** was a surprise out of left field, in that I forgot all about it after it
was announced last year. But the cast is amazing, and I am delighted to say that it was so
good that I played it all the way through in one sitting the Saturday after the last
episodes released. Then I got up on Sunday and did it again, with the other main set of
choices possible. It kicked ass. If it weren't for Hades II coming out this year, Dispatch
would be my GOTY.
* **Senua's Saga: Hellblade II** was a dissapointing way to end the year, sadly. As
expected, it was visually stunning (I'm not sure I can think of another game that takes
photorealistic environments to such heights), and the acting was excellent. Sadly, the
game is just unneccessary. I loved the first one because it's a tightly paced story with a
satisfying arc, even if the gameplay is lacking. The sequel throws away the good pacing
and character development from the first game in favor of... also having boring and
annoying gameplay. Maybe get it if it's on sale for 5 bucks, but it's not worth any more
than that.

[]($section)
## Books
My media tracker says that I read 22 books this year, which sounds about right. It may be
a handful more than that because I fucked up the tracker database partway through the year
and had to manually re-import some stuff from memory, but 22 is the number I'm going to
stick with. I definitely favored the fantasy genre (because that's the kinda guy I am),
but there's a fair smattering of other stuff in there as well. I'm not going to list them
all out here, but some particular highlights:
* The **Dungeon Crawler Carl** series is absolute top tier junk food reading, and I mean
that as a very deep compliment. I absolutey devoured all 7 books this year (pun intended),
many of them read cover-to-cover in a weekend. They might not be for everyone, but they
definitely scratched a particular itch for me.
* I read **Hyperion** for the first time this year after having it on my "Sci-Fi classics
to read" list for multiple years, and can confirm that it is excellent. The tale unfolding as a
series of intertwined novellas told during a pilgrimmage is a great framing device, and it
definitely earns its praise. I was sadly less impressed with the sequel, but maybe I'll
revist the series in the future.
* **Blood Over Brighthaven** came towards the later half of the year, when I was getting a
bit tired of the fantasy kick I'd been on for a while, and blew everything else out of the
water. It achieved the rare feat of making me actually drop my jaw at the major twist, which
gives it high points.
* I re-read **Night** in one afternoon after not reading it for over a decade. It's hard
to get through for such a thin little volume, and I had to stop at a few different points
to let myself come back to my warm, comfortable apartment and appreciate it more than I
really ever have. That sounds a little bit eye-rolling, I know, but I really think that
Night is something every single person should read, especially as an adult.

[]($section)
## Movies & TV
I made a concerted effort to not binge watch as much stuff this year, and have been
largely successful! Even though there's not much in this bucket, I do want to call out a
handful of things that really stood above for me this year:
* **A Separation** was something I watched with a few friends on one of their
recommendations, and holy *fuck* is it depressing, but holy *fuck* is it perhaps one of
the most human movies I've ever seen.
* **The Mighty Nein** mostly lived up to my expectations of it as a fan of Critical Role
(and in particular the second campaign, which I consider the peak of their production).
I'm already excited for the second season.
* I rewatched **Spinal Tap** on Christmas after having more wine than I probably should
have, and can confirm that it's top 3 funniest movies ever made. Normal movies, they don't
go to 11.

[]($section)
# On to 2026!
So there we go, another year gone. Time marches ever forward, yadda yadda yadda. I've
never been a huge fan of New Year's "Resolutions," because I feel that the term puts too
much pressure on changing too many things all at once. Instead, I like to set out New
Year's "Goals" for myself, which is largely the same thing but the semantics make me feel
better about adjusting or dropping them as the year goes on. So here's mine for 2026:

[]($section)
## Code
I write a lot of code, profesionally and as a hobby. Sue me, this gets its own section.

First off, I want to make it a rule for myself that **ALL personal projects (other than bugfixes in
existing stuff) I work on this year are in either `zig` or `gleam`** (and I may end up
dropping gleam since it's much harder to just make a static binary of a thing), with the
exception of things like nvim plugins that require a specific language. I've written about
this [before](seniorish), but I feel like my technical expertise is largely constrained to
one language (swift), with a smattering of others that I'm just OK at. I want to get
really familiar with at least one other language, and `zig` in particular presents an
opportunity to work with low level primitives while also not making me write C.

The other big thing I want to focus on this year is trimming down my dependency stack in
both my own projects and in terms of plugins and things for tools that I use. The latter
half of this year has had a couple [high-profile supply chain
attacks](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=46032539), largely from `npm` (which I
thankfully don't use), but it has made me take a more critical look at how trusting I am
with my own stuff. To use my [nvim config](https://codeberg.org/garrettm/dotfiles/src/branch/main/nvim/.config/nvim)
as an example, I currently (at time of writing and in the linked commit) have
[20 plugins installed](https://codeberg.org/garrettm/dotfiles/src/commit/0403c45fe1cf10d15f150d9019e6ae03291a7581/nvim/.config/nvim/lazy-lock.json).
In the grand scheme of nvim configs out there this is actually pretty low, but even still
it's higher than I'd like, especially since there are at least a couple in there that I
don't use super often (though you can prise `oil.nvim` from my cold, dead hands). I'd like
to take the time to narrow that set down to just the essentials, and also take the
opportunity to replace a couple of them (notably `obsidian.nvim`) with lightweight
versions that I write myself, to get familiar with the nvim lua API. Similarly, for my
personal stuff (and this is another point in the "use zig" column), I'd like to keep my
package dependencies pretty much empty, opting for code I write myself, the stdlib, or in
rare cases an established, trustworthy library like `ffmpeg`. It just seems like the thing
to do for a little while, and it'll also mean that I get some fun diversions to build my
own personal stdlib/utils library for myself going forwards.

[]($section)
## Personal
These are the real goals that I want to put some effort towards this year, stuff that I
want to look back on and be proud of.
* Off the bat, I want to stop wearing my smartwatch all the time this year. I have a
garmin watch that has basically never left my wrist since I purchased it 3ish years ago,
and though I am impressed with the overall quality of the device (barring Garmin's batshit
decision to gate their API to approved enterprise applications only), I've been steadily
finding that it puts me into a state of "analysis paralysis" about health statistics that
don't mean anything. Especially in the later half of the year, I've been putting far too
much stock in the "body battery" and "stress level" measurements that they purport to
record, and I think it's unhealthy. Besides, I have a very nice analog watch that was
a gift when I graduated college, and I'd like to go back to actually getting some style
points out of that.
* I picked up learning the guitar again towards the end of 2025 after abandoning it as a
teenager. I'm still not putting as much time into it as I'd like, so for 2026 I'm making
it a goal to be able to play [Spin the Wheel](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=opy_y0dkwmk&list=RDopy_y0dkwmk)
(or a slightly simplified version). I love the song, and it's a good mix of chording and
picking.
* On the running side of things, I want this to be the year where I finally run a 3:30
marathon. My PR is 3:55 and change, and I know I have the fitness to go significantly
faster, it's mostly about really pushing myself to stick hard to a training plan and suck
it up and do the speedwork (which I hate). I have a race in mind already for September.
* I'd like to make it a point this year to only get updates for things via RSS feeds. By
that I mean no youtube subscriptions, no reddit follows, etc, just a curated list of RSS
sources for things I actually care about to try and reduce the amount of worthless slop I
ingest into my brain. I've already started down this route and it's working pretty well,
so I'm anxious to see if I can stick to it.
* Finally, a bit of an oddball one out of left field that I have no real stake in but might
be fun: I'd like to see if I can train myself to be ambidextrous this year. I don't really
know why I want to do this, other than that it seems useful and like a neat party trick.
I'm right-handed, but for some reason choose to brush my teeth with my left hand, so how
hard can it be to do everything else with it too?

# The End Bit
It felt weird to abrubtly end on saying that I want to teach myself to be ambidextrous, so
this section is the sort of end bit that's supposed to tie it all together. I, like
everyone else on the planet, have no idea what I'm doing (except when it comes to flipping
pancakes, I'm really good at that). I hope you've all had a lovely holiday season and are
looking forward to everything the new year brings. Thanks for reading, and hi Mom!
